Look at your hand and spread your fingers. Now picture each finger many times longer, with thin skin stretched between them, down to your ankle. That is a bat's wing. The thumb stays short and free, with a claw for clinging and climbing.

Because the wing has so many bones and joints, a bat can change its shape with every beat. A bird's wing is stiffer. This is one reason bats can twist and turn so sharply after insects. Long before bats, flying [[reptile|reptiles]] called [[@pterosaurs|pterosaurs]] also flew on wings of skin, but theirs hung mainly from one enormous finger. A bat's wing skin is thin but tough, and it heals quickly if torn. It is dotted with tiny hairs that seem to help the bat feel how the air is flowing over it.

{{story:pterosaurs}}

Without insects, the world would be buried in waste. Termites and beetles break down fallen wood. Fly maggots and beetles clear away dead animals. [[@power-dung-beetle|Dung beetles]] roll, bury and eat animal droppings, putting [[nutrient|nutrients]] back into the ground.

Australia learned this the hard way. When settlers brought cattle, the native dung beetles, used to dry kangaroo droppings, could not cope with large, wet cow pats. Dung piled up on pastures and bush flies multiplied. From 1965, the Australian Dung Beetle Project brought in dozens of [[species|species]] from Africa and Europe. Many settled in, and pastures recovered.

{{story:honey}}

बैंड-मास्टर डब्ल्यू. सी. हैंडी ने बाद में लगभग 1903 की एक रात के बारे में लिखा, जब वे [[@mississippi|मिसिसिपी]] के टटवाइलर नाम की जगह के स्टेशन पर थे। लेट हुई ट्रेन का इंतज़ार करते हुए उन्होंने एक आदमी को गिटार के तारों पर चाकू दबाकर फिसलाते सुना, जिससे तार कराहने जैसी आवाज़ करते थे। वह आदमी "जहाँ सदर्न, डॉग को काटती है" वहाँ जाने के बारे में गा रहा था, यानी ऐसी जगह जहाँ दो रेल लाइनें मिलती थीं।

हैंडी ने लिखा कि इससे अजीब संगीत उन्होंने कभी नहीं सुना था। उन्होंने ब्लूज़ की धुनें लिखनी और सजानी शुरू कर दीं। उनके "मेम्फ़िस ब्लूज़" (1912) और "सेंट लुई ब्लूज़" (1914) हिट हुए, और वे ब्लूज़ के पिता कहलाने लगे।

{{story:mississippi}}

गुफ़ा-चित्र की उम्र पता करना मुश्किल है। कोयले की कार्बन डेटिंग हो सकती है, पर लाल गेरू में कार्बन नहीं होता। इसलिए वैज्ञानिक एक चतुर तरकीब अपनाते हैं। गुफ़ा की दीवारों पर टपकता पानी कैल्साइट नाम के खनिज की पतली पपड़ियाँ छोड़ जाता है। कैल्साइट में यूरेनियम की ज़रा-सी मात्रा फँस जाती है, जो एक तय रफ़्तार से थोरियम में बदलती है। अगर पपड़ी किसी चित्र के ऊपर बनी है, तो चित्र उस पपड़ी से पुराना ही होगा।

इसी तरीके से पता चला कि [[@spain|स्पेन]] की एल कास्तियो गुफ़ा में बना एक लाल गोला 40,000 साल से भी ज़्यादा पुराना है। कुछ वैज्ञानिकों का कहना है कि [[@caves-form|स्पेन की दूसरी गुफ़ाओं]] में बने निशान 64,000 साल से ज़्यादा पुराने हैं, जिसका मतलब होगा कि उन्हें [[@neanderthals|निएंडरथल]] मानवों ने बनाया। दूसरे वैज्ञानिक इन तारीख़ों पर सवाल उठाते हैं, और बहस जारी है।

{{story:stone-age}}

1947 में, जिस साल भारत आज़ाद हुआ, रज़ा ने बंबई में कुछ नौजवान चित्रकारों के साथ मिलकर प्रोग्रेसिव आर्टिस्ट्स ग्रुप बनाया। इसके सदस्यों में एफ़.एन. सूज़ा और [[@mf-husain|एम.एफ़. हुसैन]] भी थे। वे चाहते थे कि भारतीय कला बेबाक और आधुनिक हो, और पुराने ढर्रे की अंग्रेज़ी अकादमिक चित्रकला और बंगाल स्कूल की कोमल शैली, दोनों से अलग राह पकड़े।

उनका समय बहुत मायने रखता था। एक नया देश पूछ रहा था कि वह कैसा दिखना चाहता है, और इन कलाकारों के पास नए जवाब थे। इनमें से कई आगे चलकर दुनिया भर में मशहूर हुए।

{{story:circles}}

People questioned caste long before modern times. [[@buddha|The Buddha]] welcomed followers of every background. In the 12th century, [[@basavanna|Basavanna]] in Karnataka rejected caste rules. Poets like Kabir and Ravidas sang that God did not care about birth, and [[@mirabai-kabir|their verses]] are still sung today.

In 1848, Jyotirao and Savitribai Phule opened a school for girls in [[@pune|Pune]]. They later taught Dalit children too, which angered many in the city. In Kerala, Narayana Guru built temples open to all, and the Vaikom Satyagraha of 1924–25 demanded the right to walk on roads near a temple.

{{story:ambedkar}}

Barcelona is the success story people often point to. Before the 1992 Olympics, it rebuilt a run-down industrial waterfront into beaches, a marina and new neighbourhoods. Tourism grew enormously in the following decades, and the city became one of Europe's most visited.

For India, the 1982 Asian Games in Delhi brought new flyovers, stadiums and hotels. They also brought colour television: [[@tv-india|Doordarshan]] began broadcasting in colour that year so that people could watch the Games. Before the [[@commonwealth-games|2010 Commonwealth Games]], Delhi rapidly expanded its [[@metro-trains|metro]] network.

{{story:ancient-olympia}}

In many Indian fishing villages, men catch the fish and women sell it. Fisherwomen buy from the boats, then carry baskets to markets, sometimes travelling by bus or train. In Mumbai, Koli women in bright saris have sold fish in the city's markets for generations. The Kolis were among the first people to live on the seven islands that became [[@mumbai|Mumbai]].

Women also dry, salt and pickle fish. On some beaches, long rows of small fish lie drying on mats in the sun. Dried fish lasts for months and travels well, so it reaches villages far from the sea.

{{story:fishing-boats}}

In 1973, a religious leader from Kerala named Kesavananda Bharati challenged a land law. The case grew into a huge question: can [[@parliament-india|Parliament]] change any part of the Constitution it likes?

Thirteen judges heard it, the largest bench ever. By seven votes to six, they ruled that Parliament can amend the Constitution but cannot destroy its basic structure, such as [[@democracy-what|democracy]], the rule of law and fundamental rights. This idea still guides Indian law.

{{story:civil-rights}}

In the 1920s, the [[archaeologist|archaeologist]] Leonard Woolley dug up the royal tombs at Ur. He found beautiful game boards decorated with shell and red limestone, now in the British Museum. Each board had 20 squares, and players used pyramid-shaped dice.

For thousands of years nobody knew how to play. Then Irving Finkel, a curator at the British Museum, translated a clay tablet written by a Babylonian scholar in 177 BCE that explained the rules. The game had been played across the Middle East for about 3,000 years. Finkel later learned that Jewish families in [[@kochi|Kochi]], in [[@kerala|Kerala]], were still playing a version of it in the 20th century.

{{story:probability}}

Besides yellow, there are other prizes. The green jersey goes to the best sprinter, who collects points for fast finishes. The white jersey with red polka dots goes to the King of the Mountains, the best climber. The plain white jersey is for the best young rider, aged 25 or under.

Teams work together. Most riders, called domestiques, help their team leader by fetching water bottles, sheltering him from wind and setting a fast pace on climbs.

{{story:bicycle-balance}}

In the 700s and 800s, the Christian church in western Europe settled on 1 November as All Saints' Day, also called All Hallows. "Hallow" is an old word for a saint or holy person. The evening before became All Hallows' Eve, shortened over time to Halloween.

All Souls' Day, on 2 November, was set aside for praying for the dead. In parts of Britain, poor people and children went "souling", going door to door offering prayers in return for small cakes. Some historians see this as an ancestor of trick-or-treating.

{{story:bats-fly}}

1960 के दशक में गंभीर मिर्गी वाले कुछ लोगों का कॉर्पस कैलोसम काट दिया गया, ताकि दौरे फैल न सकें। अमेरिकी वैज्ञानिक रॉजर स्पेरी और उनकी टीम ने इन "स्प्लिट-ब्रेन", यानी बँटे दिमाग वाले मरीज़ों की जाँच की।

अगर कोई शब्द सिर्फ़ मरीज़ के बाएँ गोलार्ध को दिखाया जाता, तो वे उसे बोल देते। अगर सिर्फ़ दाएँ को दिखाया जाता, तो वे उसका नाम नहीं ले पाते, पर उनका बायाँ हाथ उससे मेल खाती चीज़ उठा लेता। हर आधे हिस्से के अपने हुनर लगते थे। इस काम के लिए स्पेरी को 1981 में नोबेल पुरस्कार मिला।

{{story:leonardo}}

बहुत-से रिफ़्लेक्स तुम्हें बचाते हैं। जब कोई चीज़ चेहरे की तरफ़ उड़ती आए, तो तुम [[@blinking|पलक झपकाते]] हो। खाना ग़लत नली में चला जाए, तो खाँसी आती है। गले के पिछले हिस्से को कुछ छू ले, तो उबकाई आती है। नाक साफ़ करने के लिए तुम [[@sneezes|छींकते]] हो। तेज़ रोशनी में आँखों की [[pupil|पुतलियाँ]] सिकुड़कर आँखों को बचाती हैं, और अँधेरे में चौड़ी खुल जाती हैं।

अगर तुम्हारा पैर किसी नुकीली चीज़ पर पड़ जाए, तो तुम बस वह पैर नहीं उठाते। उसी समय दूसरी टाँग अकड़कर तुम्हें थाम लेती है। यह भी एक रिफ़्लेक्स है, और यह इस बारे में सोचे बिना काम करता है कि कौन-सी टाँग क्या करे। शरीर दिन भर सैकड़ों चुपचाप चलने वाले रिफ़्लेक्स भी चलाता रहता है, जो तुम्हारी [[@heart-beat|धड़कन]], साँस और खून की नलियों का आकार ठीक करते रहते हैं, बिना तुम्हें कभी पता चले।

{{story:speed-distance}}

The finest walls at Machu Picchu are made of granite blocks, each shaped to fit its neighbours exactly. Builders pounded the stone with harder hammerstones and ground the faces smooth. They may have tried each block in place, lifted it off, trimmed it and tried again.

Walls lean slightly inwards. Doors and windows are trapezoid-shaped, wider at the bottom than the top. These shapes help the city stand firm in a land of frequent earthquakes. When the ground shakes, the stones can shift and settle back into place.

{{story:potatoes}}

Canada has two official languages, English and French. In the province of Quebec, French is the main language, and road signs, shops and schools mostly use it. Food packets all over Canada carry both languages, so a cereal box gives you a free French lesson at breakfast.

Long before either language arrived, [[indigenous|indigenous]] peoples lived here, and they still do: First Nations, Inuit and Métis. In 1999 Canada created a new [[territory|territory]] in the far north, Nunavut, where most people are Inuit and Inuktitut is an official language. Canada has also faced up to painful history, such as residential schools where indigenous children were taken from their families.

{{story:aurora}}

Jai Singh II loved astronomy. In the 1720s and 1730s he built five [[observatory|observatories]], called [[@jantar-mantar|Jantar Mantar]], in Jaipur, Delhi, [[@varanasi|Varanasi]], Ujjain and Mathura. The one in Jaipur is the largest. Its instruments are made of stone and marble, big enough to walk through.

The Samrat Yantra is a giant sundial about 27 m tall. Its shadow moves across the marked scale at about 1 mm every second, so you can watch [[@time-measure|time]] passing. [[astronomer|Astronomers]] used the instruments to track the Sun, Moon and stars, and to make calendars.

{{story:time-measure}}

Glass was heavy and broke easily on bumpy roads and rolling ships. In Britain, Peter Durand patented the use of tin-plated iron containers in 1810. Bryan Donkin and John Hall bought the idea and opened a canning factory in London around 1812 or 1813. Their cans of beef and vegetables went on navy ships and Arctic [[expedition|expeditions]].

These early cans were heavy and thick. A label from the time told people to open them with a chisel and hammer. Proper can openers only arrived in the 1850s, and the simple turning-wheel opener most kitchens use came later still.

{{story:napoleon}}

Leave a wet dough lying around and [[@food-science-bread-rise|wild yeast from the air]] will start to grow in it. The [[yeast|yeast]] eats sugars and [[@burps|burps out carbon dioxide]]. The gluten net catches the bubbles, and the dough swells. This kind of [[@fermentation|fermentation]] was probably discovered by accident.

Ancient Egyptians baked many kinds of risen bread in clay pots. Bread was so central to life that it was a kind of pay. Records and the remains of bakeries near Giza show that pyramid workers received bread and beer as part of their wages.

{{story:ancient-egypt}}
